<h1 class="gb-headline gb-headline-ba73f7d8 gb-headline-text">HTTP Status 204 (No Content)</h1>


<div class="dynamic-entry-excerpt"><p>HTTP 204 No Content explained from RFC 9110 and 9112: DELETE and PUT, ETag, no body rules, client parsing errors, testing, and mistakes.</p>
</div>

<div class="gb-container gb-container-b83a6043 inline-post-meta-area"><div class="gb-inside-container">
<img alt='Photo of author' src='https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/440b2ffa86b0f8f14d2d1e64cc0ddc8b9a2ffa2ad0fefcbe431460638bd88883?s=35&#038;d=robohash&#038;r=g' srcset='https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/440b2ffa86b0f8f14d2d1e64cc0ddc8b9a2ffa2ad0fefcbe431460638bd88883?s=70&#038;d=robohash&#038;r=g 2x' class='avatar avatar-35 photo dynamic-author-image dynamic-author-image-rounded' height='35' width='35' decoding='async'/>

<div class="gb-grid-wrapper gb-grid-wrapper-628b4c43">
<div class="gb-grid-column gb-grid-column-5d04ba48"><div class="gb-container gb-container-5d04ba48"><div class="gb-inside-container">

<p class="gb-headline gb-headline-104a6601 gb-headline-text">Written by: Lokesh Gupta</p>



<p class="gb-headline gb-headline-f06c3383 gb-headline-text">Last Updated: <time class="entry-date updated-date" datetime="2026-10-01T12:28:25+05:30">October 1, 2026</time></p>

</div></div></div>
</div>
</div></div>
</div></div></div>
</div>
</div></div>

<div class="gb-container gb-container-efa2e9db hide-on-desktop hide-on-tablet"><div class="gb-inside-container">
<img width="1440" height="1260" src="https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag.png" class="dynamic-featured-image wp-post-image" alt="Four step diagram of a PUT request with If-Match that returns 204 No Content and a new ETag, which the client stores for the next update" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag.png 1440w, https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag-300x263.png 300w, https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag-1024x896.png 1024w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" />
</div></div>
	<div class="site grid-container container hfeed" id="page">
				<div class="site-content" id="content">
			
	<div class="content-area" id="primary">
		<main class="site-main" id="main">
			
<article id="post-156" class="post-156 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-http-status mv-content-wrapper" itemtype="https://schema.org/CreativeWork" itemscope>
	<div class="inside-article">
		
		<div class="entry-content" itemprop="text">
			
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">HTTP status <em>204 (No Content)</em> means the server fulfilled the request and has nothing to send back in the response content. It is the usual answer to a <em>DELETE</em>, and to a <em>PUT</em> or <em>PATCH</em> when the client does not need the updated resource. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A complete <em>204</em> response is only a status line and headers:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
Date: Thu, 01 Oct 2026 10:15:00 GMT</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The rules below come from <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110</a> (HTTP Semantics) and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9112</a> (HTTP/1.1), which replaced RFC 7231 and RFC 7230, and each rule links to its section.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">1. Rules for Using HTTP 204 (No Content)</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The table below lists the rules for a <em>204</em> response. </p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Strength</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A <em>204</em> response means the server fulfilled the request and has no additional content to send.</td><td>Definition</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 15.3.5</a></td></tr><tr><td>Header fields in a <em>204</em> response describe the target resource after the action was applied. An <em>ETag</em> after a <em>PUT</em> is the tag of the new representation.</td><td>Definition</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 15.3.5</a></td></tr><tr><td>The client does not need to leave its current view. It shows its own success indication and applies any new header metadata.</td><td>Definition</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 15.3.5</a></td></tr><tr><td>A <em>204</em> response ends at the end of the header section and cannot contain content or trailers.</td><td>Definition</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 15.3.5</a></td></tr><tr><td>A <em>204</em> response is heuristically cacheable unless the method definition or cache controls say otherwise.</td><td>Definition</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 15.3.5</a></td></tr><tr><td>A server must not send a <em>Content-Length</em> header in a <em>204</em> response.</td><td>MUST NOT</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 8.6</a></td></tr><tr><td>A server must not send a <em>Transfer-Encoding</em> header in a <em>204</em> response.</td><td>MUST NOT</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-6.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">9112 Section 6.1</a></td></tr><tr><td>A <em>204</em> response always ends at the first empty line after the headers, whatever headers it carries.</td><td>MUST</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-6.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">9112 Section 6.3</a></td></tr><tr><td>A <em>PUT</em> that modifies an existing resource must send <em>200</em> or <em>204</em>.</td><td>MUST</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.3.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 9.3.4</a></td></tr><tr><td>A successful <em>PUT</em> response must not carry an <em>ETag</em> or <em>Last-Modified</em> unless the stored content is identical to what the client sent.</td><td>MUST NOT</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.3.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 9.3.4</a></td></tr><tr><td>A successful <em>DELETE</em> should send <em>202</em>, <em>204</em> or <em>200</em>, depending on whether the deletion is pending, finished without details, or finished with a status description.</td><td>SHOULD</td><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Section 9.3.5</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">2. HTTP 204 Request and Response Examples</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2.1. DELETE Returns 204</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Suppose a project tracking API lets users delete a project. The server removes the project and has nothing useful to report, so it answers <em>204</em>.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">DELETE /projects/42 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;

HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
Date: Thu, 01 Oct 2026 10:15:00 GMT</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The response has no <em>Content-Type</em>, no <em>Content-Length</em> and no body. None of them applies to a response that has no content.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2.2. PUT Returns 204 with an ETag</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A client updates a project and includes the <em>ETag</em> it last saw in an <em>If-Match</em> header. The server stores the data exactly as received, so it may send the new <em>ETag</em> without any body.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">PUT /projects/42 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: "v7"

{ "name": "Apollo", "status": "active" }

HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
ETag: "v8"</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the server had changed the data while storing it, for example by adding an <em>updatedAt</em> value, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.3.4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">section 9.3.4</a> forbids sending the validator. In that case it should answer <em>200 OK</em> with the stored representation.</p>


<div class="wp-block-image">
<figure class="aligncenter size-full is-resized"><img decoding="async" width="1440" height="1260" src="https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag.png" alt="Four step diagram of a PUT request with If-Match that returns 204 No Content and a new ETag, which the client stores for the next update" class="wp-image-2256" style="width:724px;height:auto" srcset="https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag.png 1440w, https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag-300x263.png 300w, https://restfulapi.net/wp-content/uploads/http-204-put-etag-1024x896.png 1024w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 1: A 204 response can carry the new ETag without any body.</figcaption></figure>
</div>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">3. Choosing Between 204 and Similar Responses</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Status code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The action succeeded and the client needs nothing back</td><td><em>204 No Content</em></td></tr><tr><td>The action succeeded and the response returns the updated or deleted resource</td><td><em>200 OK</em></td></tr><tr><td>The deletion has been accepted but is not finished</td><td><em>202 Accepted</em></td></tr><tr><td>The action succeeded and the client should reset its form or view</td><td><em>205 Reset Content</em></td></tr><tr><td>A new resource was created</td><td><em>201 Created</em></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The difference between <em>204</em> and <em>205</em> is often missed. A <em>204</em> tells the client to keep its current view, while a <em>205</em> asks it to reset the view, for example by clearing a form (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">section 15.3.6</a>). A server must not send content with a <em>205</em> either.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">4. Problem: Clients Fail on the Empty Body</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The most common <em>204</em> bug is on the client side. Code that always parses the response as JSON throws an error on an empty body, even though the request succeeded. In JavaScript, calling <em>response.json()</em> on a <em>204</em> response fails with an error such as &#8220;Unexpected end of JSON input&#8221; (see <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN</a>). In Java, parsing an empty string with Jackson fails with a &#8220;No content to map&#8221; error.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4.1. Solution: Check the Status Before Parsing</h3>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">async function request(url, options) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Request failed: ' + response.status);
  if (response.status === 204) return null;
  return response.json();
}</code></pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4.2. Solution: Use a Bodyless API in Java</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With Spring&#8217;s <em>WebClient</em>, we call <em>toBodilessEntity()</em> instead of <em>bodyToMono()</em> when we expect a <em>204</em>, so no body conversion is attempted.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">webClient.delete()
    .uri("/projects/{id}", 42)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity()
    .block();</code></pre>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">5. Problem: Sending a Body or Length with 204</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some servers add <em>Content-Length: 0</em> or even a small body to a <em>204</em> response. RFC 9110 and RFC 9112 forbid the length and transfer headers, and a <em>204</em> always ends at the first empty line after the headers. A receiver therefore ignores any bytes after that point. If a proxy or client reads them as the start of the next response, the connection gets out of step. The solution is to send nothing after the headers and to leave out <em>Content-Length</em>, <em>Transfer-Encoding</em> and <em>Content-Type</em>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">6. DELETE Policies: 204, 200 or 202</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">RFC 9110 allows three answers to a successful <em>DELETE</em>, so the API should pick one rule and document it. Use <em>204</em> when the project is gone and nothing more needs saying. Use <em>200</em> when the response describes the result, such as a summary of what was deleted. Use <em>202</em> when deletion happens later, and point to a status resource as described in the article on <a href="https://restfulapi.net/http-status-202-accepted/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTP Status 202 (Accepted)</a>. A repeated <em>DELETE</em> is idempotent in effect, but the second response is often <em>404</em>, and the documentation should say whether the API prefers <em>404</em> or <em>204</em> for it.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">7. Preflight Requests and 204</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Browsers send a preflight <em>OPTIONS</em> request before some cross-origin calls, and many servers answer it with <em>204</em> plus the <em>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</em>, <em>Access-Control-Allow-Methods</em> and <em>Access-Control-Allow-Headers</em> headers. This is a convention, and the meaning of <em>204</em> is the same as before: the request worked and there is nothing to send back.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">8. Code Examples for Returning 204</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We did not run these snippets as part of this article, so adapt them to your own project.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">// Spring Boot
@DeleteMapping("/projects/{id}")
public ResponseEntity&lt;Void&gt; delete(@PathVariable long id) {
    service.delete(id);
    return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
}</code></pre>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">// Node.js with Express
app.delete('/projects/:id', async (req, res) =&gt; {
  await service.remove(req.params.id);
  res.status(204).end();
});</code></pre>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class=""># Python with Flask
@app.delete("/projects/&lt;int:project_id&gt;")
def delete_project(project_id):
    service.remove(project_id)
    return "", 204</code></pre>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">9. How to Test a 204 Response</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A test for a <em>204</em> response checks the status and also checks that nothing else arrives. The table lists what to assert. As with the code above, we did not run the commands against a live server, so adjust the URLs to your own API.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>What to check</th><th>Expected result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Status line</td><td><em>204 No Content</em></td></tr><tr><td>Body</td><td>Empty</td></tr><tr><td><em>Content-Length</em> and <em>Transfer-Encoding</em> headers</td><td>Absent</td></tr><tr><td><em>ETag</em> header after a <em>PUT</em></td><td>Present only when the stored data equals the request data</td></tr><tr><td>A second <em>DELETE</em> of the same project</td><td>The documented policy, such as <em>404</em></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9.1. Test with curl</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <em>-i</em> option prints the status line and headers, so we can confirm that no body or length header follows.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">curl -i -X DELETE https://api.example.com/projects/42 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;"</code></pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9.2. Test with Postman</h3>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">pm.test("Status is 204 No Content", function () {
  pm.response.to.have.status(204);
});

pm.test("Body is empty", function () {
  pm.expect(pm.response.text()).to.eql("");
});

pm.test("No Content-Length header", function () {
  pm.expect(pm.response.headers.has("Content-Length")).to.be.false;
});</code></pre>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9.3. Automated Tests in Code</h3>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">// Spring MockMvc
mockMvc.perform(delete("/projects/42"))
    .andExpect(status().isNoContent())
    .andExpect(content().string(""));</code></pre>



<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="">// Jest with Supertest
it('deletes the project', async () =&gt; {
  const res = await request(app).delete('/projects/42').expect(204);
  expect(res.text).toBe('');
});</code></pre>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">10. Common Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Sending a JSON body with a <em>204</em> response breaks the framing rule in <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-6.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9112 section 6.3</a>, because receivers stop reading at the end of the headers.</li>



<li>Sending <em>Content-Length: 0</em> with a <em>204</em> response breaks the <em>MUST NOT</em> rule in <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">section 8.6</a>. Leave the header out.</li>



<li>Returning <em>204</em> from a <em>GET</em> that should return data leaves clients with nothing to show. Use <em>200</em> with an empty list or object instead.</li>



<li>Sending an <em>ETag</em> after a <em>PUT</em> that changed the stored data breaks the rule in <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.3.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">section 9.3.4</a>. Return <em>200</em> with the stored representation instead.</li>



<li>Parsing every response as JSON on the client makes every <em>204</em> look like a failure, so the client has to check the status first.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">11. Summary</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A <em>204 (No Content)</em> response says that the action worked and that there is nothing to return. It ends at the headers, carries no <em>Content-Length</em> or <em>Transfer-Encoding</em>, may carry an <em>ETag</em> that describes the new state, and fits <em>DELETE</em> and <em>PUT</em> well. Clients must check for it before parsing a body.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">12. References</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.3.5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics, section 15.3.5 (204 No Content)</a></li>



<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-6.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9112: HTTP/1.1, section 6.3 (Message Body Length)</a></li>



<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 5789: PATCH Method for HTTP</a></li>



<li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status/204" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN Web Docs: 204 No Content</a></li>



<li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN Web Docs: Response.json()</a></li>



<li><a href="https://restfulapi.net/http-status-201-created/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTP Status 201 (Created)</a></li>



<li><a href="https://restfulapi.net/http-status-202-accepted/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTP Status 202 (Accepted)</a></li>



<li><a href="https://restfulapi.net/http-status-200-ok/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTP Status Code 200 (OK)</a></li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Happy Learning !!</p>
